Yes. The problem is usually not just “they make excuses.” It is that their mind may be running a standing search query:

What lets me avoid the uncomfortable conclusion/action?

Once that search is active, the brain becomes very good at finding candidate justifications.

The fix is to change the decision architecture before the moment arrives:

define the standard in advance
→ define what would count as a legitimate exception
→ notice when a reason appears
→ ask whether it was predictable beforehand
→ separate explanation from permission
→ still do the smallest required action when possible

A useful test is:

Would I accept this reason if someone else gave it to me?

And another:

Did this fact actually make the action impossible, or did it merely make avoidance easier to justify?

You can also prevent the opposite error—assuming every reason is an excuse—by keeping two buckets:

constraint
vs.
rationalization

Then require evidence before moving something into either one.

So the operator is Excuse-Proofing:

precommit criteria → expose rationalization → preserve real exceptions → act on what remains

The deeper goal is not “never explain yourself.” It is to stop allowing the explanation to quietly rewrite the standard after the fact.